## TLS — soft builtins (real crypto)

Windows uses **SChannel** (always). Linux uses **OpenSSL** when compiled with
`-DRYNIX_RT_OPENSSL` and linked `-lssl -lcrypto`. Otherwise the builtins
return `-2` (unsupported).

`tls_serve_once_echo(port) -> i64`

One-shot TLS server: listen → accept → handshake → decrypt one payload →
encrypt the same bytes back. Returns `0` on success, `-1` on error, `-2` if
no TLS backend.

`tls_client_echo(host, port, msg) -> i64`

TLS client: connect → handshake → encrypt `msg` → expect identical decrypt.
Returns `0` on match.

Self-signed certificates are generated at runtime (smoke skips peer
validation). This is **not** production PKI — it proves real TLS framing
unlike simulated “TLS” stubs.

Evidence: `rt/tests/tls_echo_smoke.c` / `size_echo_gates::tls_echo_smoke_c`.

HTTP JSON over the same TLS backends lives in soft HTTP
(`http_tls_serve_once_json_i64` / `http_tls_get_json_i64`; see `std/http.ryx`
and `http_tls_product_smoke`).
